\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


A system is a probability space $(X,\mathcal X,\mu)$ with invertible measure-preserving transformations; multiple transformations are assumed to commute. Write $T^j f=f\circ T^j$, and $T^j A$ for the image of a measurable set under $T^j$. A system with one transformation is ergodic if every invariant measurable set has measure zero or one. The space $L^\infty(\mu)$ consists of essentially bounded complex measurable functions. Choose distinct $a,b\in(0,\infty)\setminus\mathbb Z$. Here $\lfloor t\rfloor$ denotes the largest integer at most $t$.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Does every ergodic system and every pair $f,g\in L^\infty(\mu)$ satisfy \[\lim_{N\to\infty}\frac1N\sum_{n=1}^N f(T^{\lfloor n^a\rfloor}x)g(T^{\lfloor n^b\rfloor}x)=\left(\int_X f\,d\mu\right)\left(\int_X g\,d\mu\right)\] for $\mu$-almost every $x$? \href{https://arxiv.org/pdf/1103.3808v3}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Do two distinct positive noninteger powers give almost-everywhere convergence to the product of means in every ergodic system?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, \emph{UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems}, record 4800024 (AMR-047-0024). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] N. Frantzikinakis, Some open problems on multiple ergodic averages (2016), Section 6.1.3, full Problem 24, p.32. \url{https://arxiv.org/pdf/1103.3808v3}.
\end{description}
